# math — mathematical constants (data-only stdlib module)
#
# The reference's spellings come first and are the literal: `import math` / `print(math.pi)` is a program
# CPython runs, and the conformance leg can only adjudicate a name it can also spell (roadmap L11.6, the
# "typed stdlib constants" clause; ADR 0272 gave the value its type and ADR 0321 gives the name its twin).
# The upper-case names are this language's own — the ones `docs/language.md` documents and the record holds —
# and they are ALIASES of the same declaration rather than a second literal, so the two spellings of a
# constant cannot drift: one number, two names, one place where it is written.
pi = 3.141592653589793
e = 2.718281828459045
tau = 6.283185307179586
PI = pi
E = e
TAU = tau
# The four names below are this language's extension: the reference's `math` module does not carry them, so
# there is no CPython program to compare them with and they are asserted against the values themselves
# (integration/math_const_spelling_test.go). Their lower-case spellings exist so a reader who reaches for the
# reference's style is not surprised.
PHI = 1.618033988749895
SQRT2 = 1.4142135623730951
LN2 = 0.6931471805599453
LN10 = 2.302585092994046
phi = PHI
sqrt2 = SQRT2
ln2 = LN2
ln10 = LN10
